---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocPageShell from "@/components/DocPageShell.astro";
import Callout from "@/components/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
---
<DocsLayout
	title="Agent tools — ayjnt docs"
	description="Per-route tool collections a model can call: tools.ts runs in workerd, tools.host.ts runs in the Bun host process, and both merge into one ToolSet."
>
	<DocPageShell
		slug="guides/agent-tools"
		lede="Drop a tools.ts next to an agent and the model gets tools that run in workerd. Drop a tools.host.ts and it gets tools that run on the local machine. The filename picks the runtime."
	>
		<h2>Two files, two runtimes</h2>
		<p>
			A tool collection is co-located with the agent that owns it, the same
			way <code>app.tsx</code> and <code>docs.md</code> are. There are two
			filenames, and which one you use decides where the function body
			executes.
		</p>
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th>File</th>
					<th>Runs in</th>
					<th>Can use</th>
					<th>Deploys?</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>agents/&lt;route&gt;/tools.ts</code>
					</td>
					<td>workerd, next to the agent</td>
					<td>
						The agent's own state, <code>fetch</code>, plain
						computation
					</td>
					<td>Yes — like any other worker code</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>agents/&lt;route&gt;/tools.host.ts</code>
					</td>
					<td>The Bun host process</td>
					<td>
						<code>Bun.$</code>, <code>Bun.file</code>,{" "}
						<code>bun:sqlite</code>, node APIs
					</td>
					<td>
						No — <code>ayjnt deploy</code> refuses
					</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		<p>
			The rule of thumb: if a tool only needs the agent's state, the
			network, or arithmetic, it belongs in <code>tools.ts</code>. Reach for{" "}
			<code>tools.host.ts</code> only when a tool genuinely needs the local
			machine.
		</p>

		<h3>Workerd-side tools</h3>
		<p>
			Export AI-SDK tools built with <code>tool()</code> from{" "}
			<code>"ai"</code>. Nothing about them is ayjnt-specific — a{" "}
			<code>tools.ts</code> is a plain module of AI-SDK tool definitions.
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			filename="agents/notes/tools.ts"
			lang="ts"
			code={`import { tool } from "ai";
import { z } from "zod";

export const countWords = tool({
  description: "Count the words in a piece of text.",
  inputSchema: z.object({ text: z.string() }),
  execute: async ({ text }) => ({
    words: text.trim().split(" ").filter(Boolean).length,
    characters: text.length,
  }),
});`}
		/>

		<h3>Host-side tools</h3>
		<p>
			Export <code>hostTool()</code> definitions from{" "}
			<code>"ayjnt/tools"</code>. Same shape, plus one required field —{" "}
			<code>sideEffects</code>, covered below.
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			filename="agents/notes/tools.host.ts"
			lang="ts"
			code={`import { confinePath, hostTool } from "ayjnt/tools";
import { z } from "zod";

const ROOT = process.cwd();

export const readProjectFile = hostTool({
  description: "Read a UTF-8 text file from the project directory.",
  sideEffects: "read",
  inputSchema: z.object({ path: z.string() }),
  execute: async ({ path }: { path: string }) => {
    const file = Bun.file(confinePath(ROOT, path));
    if (!(await file.exists())) throw new Error(\`no such file: \${path}\`);
    return { path, text: await file.text() };
  },
});`}
			highlightLines={[8, 11]}
		/>
		<p>
			The <code>execute</code> body never reaches workerd. The host imports
			this module directly; the worker only ever receives{" "}
			<code>description</code> and <code>inputSchema</code> so it can
			advertise the tool to the model and proxy calls back.
		</p>

		<h2>Why there is no “use host” directive</h2>
		<p>
			You might expect a <code>"use host"</code> pragma at the top of the
			file, by analogy with React's <code>"use client"</code> and{" "}
			<code>"use server"</code>. There deliberately isn't one:{" "}
			<strong>the filename is the declaration.</strong>
		</p>
		<p>
			React needs a directive because it has no filename convention — any
			module can be imported from either environment, so the boundary has to
			be written inside the file. ayjnt already has a filename convention
			for everything else in the tree (<code>agent.ts</code>,{" "}
			<code>middleware.ts</code>, <code>app.tsx</code>), so a directive would
			be redundant ceremony that can <em>disagree</em> with the path. One
			source of truth, and it's visible in a directory listing.
		</p>

		<h2>Using them from the agent</h2>
		<p>
			Both kinds converge on the AI-SDK <code>ToolSet</code> shape, so they
			merge into one object at the call site and spread alongside{" "}
			<a href="/docs/guides/browser" class="link-underline">
				browser tools
			</a>
			:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			filename="agents/notes/agent.ts"
			lang="ts"
			code={`import { agentTools } from "ayjnt/tools";
import { browserTools } from "ayjnt/browser";
import { generateText } from "ai";

const tools = { ...browserTools(this), ...agentTools(this) };
const result = await generateText({ model, tools, messages });`}
			highlightLines={[5]}
		/>
		<p>
			<code>agentTools(this)</code> returns the agent's own workerd-side
			tools plus proxies for any host tools the local runtime advertised.
			Nothing in the agent knows about the split — from its point of view
			they're all just tools. It returns <code>{"{}"}</code> when an agent
			has no tools at all, so it is always safe to spread.
		</p>

		<h2>Tool names the model sees</h2>
		<p>
			Workerd-side tools keep their export names. Host tools are{" "}
			<strong>namespaced by route</strong>, so two agents can each have a{" "}
			<code>search</code> tool without colliding:
		</p>
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th>Export</th>
					<th>Name the model sees</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>readProjectFile</code> in{" "}
						<code>agents/notes/tools.host.ts</code>
					</td>
					<td>
						<code>notes__readProjectFile</code>
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>list</code> in{" "}
						<code>agents/admin/users/tools.host.ts</code>
					</td>
					<td>
						<code>admin_users__list</code>
					</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		<p>
			Nested routes flatten with a single underscore and the route is
			separated from the export name by a double underscore. Both are
			forced by provider-side tool-name rules — most model APIs accept only{" "}
			<code>[a-zA-Z0-9_-]</code>, so slashes can't survive. If a
			workerd-side tool and a host tool ever produce the same key, the
			workerd one wins: it lives in the same file tree as the agent and
			deploys with it, which makes it the less surprising winner.
		</p>

		<h2>The build-time guard</h2>
		<p>
			A <code>tools.ts</code> that reaches for a Bun-only API fails the
			build, with a pointer at the file you probably meant to write:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			lang="sh"
			code={`$ ayjnt build
agents/notes/tools.ts: uses \`Bun.file\`, which does not exist in workerd.
tools.ts runs inside the Workers runtime alongside your agent. Rename the file
to tools.host.ts to run these functions on the Bun host instead (note: host
tools cannot be deployed to Cloudflare).`}
		/>
		<p>
			The alternative was letting it compile and then throwing{" "}
			<code>Bun is not defined</code> inside workerd on the first tool call,
			with nothing pointing at the cause. The check looks for{" "}
			<code>Bun.*</code> member access and <code>bun:</code> module
			specifiers. Comments are stripped before checking, so a doc comment
			mentioning <code>Bun.$</code> is fine —{" "}
			<code>node:</code> imports are fine too, since{" "}
			<code>nodejs_compat</code> is always on in generated configs.
		</p>

		<h2>sideEffects and permission</h2>
		<p>
			Every host tool must declare what it does. There is no default,
			because a default would silently pick a trust level on the author's
			behalf.
		</p>
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th><code>sideEffects</code></th>
					<th>Behaviour</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>"read"</code>
					</td>
					<td>Runs freely.</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>"write"</code>
					</td>
					<td>
						Needs <code>--allow-host-writes</code>.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>"exec"</code>
					</td>
					<td>
						Needs <code>--allow-host-exec</code>.
					</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		<p>
			Permission can also come from{" "}
			<code>AYJNT_ALLOW_HOST_EFFECTS=write,exec</code> — a comma list, so a
			compiled binary can run non-interactively in CI without being rebuilt
			with different flags. Failing both of those, and only when stdin is a
			TTY, the host asks:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			lang="sh"
			code={`[ayjnt] An agent wants to run host tool "appendToLog" (/notes),
        declared sideEffects: "write".
        Arguments came from model output. Allow for this session? [y/N]`}
		/>
		<p>
			An answer is remembered for the rest of the session, per tool. An
			agent loop can call the same tool dozens of times, and re-prompting
			each time would train you to approve without reading. With no TTY and
			no grant, the call is refused with a message naming the flag that
			would allow it.
		</p>

		<h2>Security: model output reaches your machine</h2>
		<Callout kind="danger" title="A host tool is a path from untrusted text to local code execution">
			<p>
				The arguments to a host tool come from{" "}
				<strong>model output</strong>, and that output may have been
				shaped by content the agent ingested — an inbound email, a
				retrieved document, a fetched page. Anyone who can get text in
				front of your agent has an indirect influence on the arguments
				your host tools receive.
			</p>
			<p>
				This is not hypothetical prompt-injection theory: it is the
				normal operating condition of an agent that reads things.
				Treat every host tool input as attacker-controlled.
			</p>
		</Callout>
		<p>Three mitigations, in order of how often they matter:</p>
		<h3>1. Never build shell commands by string</h3>
		<p>
			<code>Bun.$</code> escapes its interpolations by default, so{" "}
			<code>await Bun.$`git -C {"${dir}"} log`</code> is safe even when{" "}
			<code>dir</code> is hostile — it becomes one argument, not a command
			separator. What breaks that is{" "}
			<code>Bun.$(rawString)</code>, which takes a pre-built command line
			and hands it to the shell unescaped. Don't use it in a host tool.
		</p>
		<h3>2. Confine every model-supplied path</h3>
		<p>
			“The model passed a path” is the most common way a host tool turns
			into a file-disclosure primitive. Use <code>confinePath</code> from{" "}
			<code>"ayjnt/tools"</code>:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			lang="ts"
			code={`import { confinePath } from "ayjnt/tools";

// Throws if the resolved path escapes ROOT.
execute: async ({ file }: { file: string }) =>
  await Bun.file(confinePath(ROOT, file)).text(),`}
		/>
		<p>
			A <code>startsWith</code> check on the raw string is <em>not</em>{" "}
			enough, and it fails in two different directions:{" "}
			<code>../../.ssh/id_rsa</code> passes it because <code>..</code>{" "}
			segments have to be resolved before comparing, and a sibling
			directory like <code>/srv/data-evil</code> passes it because it
			shares a prefix with <code>/srv/data</code>.{" "}
			<code>confinePath</code> resolves first, then compares the relative
			path, which handles both. It's pure path arithmetic, so it works from
			either runtime.
		</p>
		<h3>3. Declare the narrowest sideEffects that works</h3>
		<p>
			A tool that only reads should say <code>"read"</code>; a tool that
			shells out should say <code>"exec"</code> even if today's command is
			harmless. The declaration is what the permission gate has to work
			with, and over-declaring trains users to grant more than they need.
		</p>

		<h2>Deploying a project with host tools</h2>
		<p>
			<code>ayjnt deploy</code> refuses outright:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			lang="sh"
			code={`$ ayjnt deploy
cannot deploy: 1 host tool file(s) would not work in production.

  agents/notes/tools.host.ts  (/notes)

Host tools run in the Bun process that hosts your local runtime. A deployed
Cloudflare worker has no host process, so these functions have nowhere to run.`}
		/>
		<p>
			The three alternatives were to omit the tools silently, to deploy
			stubs that throw on first call, or to fail here. Failing is the only
			one that surfaces the problem while it's still cheap — a silent
			omission gives the same agent different capabilities in production
			than locally with nothing to indicate it, and a throwing stub
			converts a build-time error into a production incident. Your options:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Move the tools into <code>tools.ts</code> to run them in workerd
				(they lose <code>Bun.$</code>, <code>Bun.file</code>,{" "}
				<code>bun:sqlite</code> and node APIs).
			</li>
			<li>
				Ship the app with{" "}
				<a href="/docs/guides/compile" class="link-underline">
					<code>ayjnt compile</code>
				</a>{" "}
				instead of deploying it.
			</li>
			<li>
				If the agent works without them, add the comment marker{" "}
				<code>@ayjnt-optional-on-deploy</code> anywhere in the file. Its
				tools are then omitted from the deployed ToolSet instead of
				blocking the deploy.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<Callout kind="note" title="Degrades, doesn't error">
			<p>
				Deployed, no bridge is bound, so <code>agentTools()</code>{" "}
				returns only the workerd-side tools. An agent that was written
				to tolerate their absence keeps working with a smaller ToolSet —
				which is exactly what makes{" "}
				<code>@ayjnt-optional-on-deploy</code> safe rather than a way to
				defer a crash.
			</p>
		</Callout>

		<h2>How the bridge works</h2>
		<p>
			Worth knowing if you're debugging, and shorter than you'd expect.
		</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				At boot, the host imports every <code>tools.host.ts</code>,
				reads each <code>hostTool()</code> export, and converts its
				schema to JSON Schema (zod 4's <code>z.toJSONSchema</code>; a
				plain JSON Schema passes through untouched). Schemas are always
				in sync with the implementation because they're derived from it,
				not restated.
			</li>
			<li>
				The descriptors —{" "}
				<code>{"{ route, name, toolName, description, sideEffects, inputSchema }"}</code>{" "}
				— cross into workerd as a plain JSON binding. The worker builds a{" "}
				<code>dynamicTool()</code> proxy for each, which is the AI-SDK
				primitive for a tool whose schema is only known at runtime.
			</li>
			<li>
				Calls travel back over Miniflare's{" "}
				<code>serviceBindings</code>: the framework registers a plain
				host function, Miniflare runs it in the Bun process and exposes
				it to workerd as an ordinary <code>Fetcher</code>. So{" "}
				<code>env.__AYJNT_HOST.fetch(...)</code> inside workerd lands on
				Bun code.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			There is no capnweb or Cap'n Proto dependency here — workerd already
			speaks Cap'n Proto to that loopback internally, so the capability
			channel exists and all the framework adds is a payload format on top
			of it. The payload is JSON because these are LLM tool calls:
			arguments arrive as JSON from the model and results go back into the
			model's context as JSON. Streaming, callback arguments, or promise
			pipelining would justify reaching for something richer; tool calls do
			not.
		</p>
		<p>
			Tool failures come back as a structured{" "}
			<code>{"{ ok: false, error }"}</code> rather than a bridge fault — a
			model passing a bad path or a command exiting non-zero is expected
			traffic, and the model can usually recover if you hand it the
			message.
		</p>

		<h2>Where to go next</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<a href="/docs/guides/cli-file" class="link-underline">
					The cli.ts file
				</a>{" "}
				— drive agents and their tools from a Bun foreground process.
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="/docs/guides/compile" class="link-underline">
					Compiling to an executable
				</a>{" "}
				— the shipping story for an app with host tools.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</DocPageShell>
</DocsLayout>
